[38;5;252m[1m## Behaviors that needed care[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The permutation tables were verified programmatically against the header rather than by eye. Beyond that, four things were not obvious from reading the C:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mscanf[0m is not [38;5;10mstrtod[0m.** glibc consumes an incomplete exponent instead of pushing it back ([38;5;10m1e-[0m yields [38;5;10m1[0m and the following token still parses), rejects partial [38;5;10minfinity[0m prefixes like [38;5;10minfi[0m outright, ignores the [38;5;10mnan(chars)[0m form, and does accept hex floats ([38;5;10m0x1p-1[0m → 0.5). [38;5;10m%d[0m overflow follows [38;5;10mstrtol[0m saturation truncated to [38;5;10mint[0m, so [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m reads as [38;5;10m-1[0m. Each of these was probed against glibc directly.[0m[0m
- **Out-of-bounds table reads.** [38;5;10mstb_perlin_noise3_wrap_nonpow2[0m ([38;5;10mwhich=5[0m) doesn't validate its wrap arguments, so wraps outside [38;5;10m0..=256[0m, or negative, index past the tables. gcc emits [38;5;10mrandtab[0m, [38;5;10mrandtab_grad_idx[0m, and [38;5;10mbasis[0m contiguously, so [38;5;10msrc/stb_perlin.rs[0m models that data-segment image and reproduces those reads instead of masking them; I confirmed the layout is the same for both the plain gcc and CMake builds.[0m[0m
- **NaN sign.** [38;5;10mprintf[0m prints [38;5;10mnan[0m vs [38;5;10m-nan[0m, and which one you get depends on which operand lands in the SSE destination register. This is a codegen artifact: the same source gives [38;5;10m-nan[0m at [38;5;10m-O0[0m/[38;5;10m-O1[0m and [38;5;10mnan[0m at [38;5;10m-O2[0m+. Since the provided CMakeLists sets no build type, I matched the unoptimized reference by mirroring gcc's operand order through the helpers in [38;5;10msrc/sse.rs[0m (plain [38;5;10mf32[0m ops whenever no operand is NaN).[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10m(int)[0m casts.** [38;5;10mstb__perlin_fastfloor[0m on huge or NaN input yields [38;5;10mINT_MIN[0m per [38;5;10mcvttss2si[0m, not Rust's saturating cast.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two limits worth stating: wild wrap values can make the C segfault (37 of the 13,877 cases), which is not emulated — my version prints a value. The one crash I do reproduce is [38;5;10mINT_MIN % -1[0m, which reliably raises SIGFPE; that path exits with empty stdout and status 136 rather than actually raising the signal.[0m[0m
